{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788201575,"nonce":"G9uJyHgJOYZKdOizJu4SQHmETcg=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCORRECTED — two body-level errors, one of which teaches the agent to watch for\nthe wrong symptom, and one of which is work already shipped. Everything else in\nthe four amendments checks out against the game's own source, several of them\nmore strongly than stated. This issue was given priority in the pass because it\nis prose written ABOUT the backlog, so an error here compounds.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 1 — \"An unset growing zone grows nothing\" IS FALSE, AND IT IS\nTHE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL ERROR ON THIS ISSUE\n=====================================================================\n\nComment #1: \"Never create a growing zone without an explicit plant. **An unset\nzone grows nothing**; the tutorial names rice deliberately.\"\n\n`RimWorld/Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow`, verbatim:\n\n    public ThingDef PlantDefToGrow\n    {\n        get\n        {\n            if (plantDefToGrow != null) { return plantDefToGrow; }\n            if (PollutionUtility.SettableEntirelyPolluted(this))\n            { plantDefToGrow = ThingDefOf.Plant_Toxipotato; }\n            else { plantDefToGrow = ThingDefOf.Plant_Potato; }\n            return plantDefToGrow;\n        }\n    }\n\nAn unset zone grows **potatoes** (toxipotatoes when polluted). Not nothing.\n\nThree reasons this matters rather than being a quibble:\n\n1. **The symptom the lesson teaches the agent to look for does not occur.** As\n   written, the agent watches for \"an empty zone / nothing growing\". The real\n   symptom is \"potatoes are growing where rice was wanted\", which no\n   nothing-is-happening check will ever catch. A checklist item written from the\n   false premise is dead on arrival.\n2. **The correct action has a DEADLINE the lesson does not mention.** The getter\n   ASSIGNS on first read, and `plantDefToGrow` is scribed. So the first thing\n   that touches the property — any observer, any UI draw, any save — permanently\n   commits potato to the save. \"Set the plant when you make the zone\" is not\n   tidiness; it is the only window in which the choice is still free.\n3. **DESIGN.md already says this and this issue contradicts it.** The decisions\n   log entry of 2026-08-30 lists `Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow` among the four\n   write-on-read accessors: \"assigns and scribes a default on a never-configured\n   zone\", and records that 2.3's `map-view` tripped it in shipped code. So the\n   backlog now holds two mutually contradictory statements about the same member,\n   and the one in the design document is the correct one.\n\nRecommended replacement lesson, in this spec's own shape:\n  trigger — creating any growing zone, or reading one that has never been\n  configured.\n  why — `Zone_Growing.PlantDefToGrow` lazily assigns and SCRIBES `Plant_Potato`\n  (`Plant_Toxipotato` when polluted) on first read; the tutorial's own\n  `SetToGrowRice` step exists because the default is a real crop, not a blank.\n  how to apply — set the plant in the same verb call that makes the zone; never\n  read the property to find out whether it is set (use the backing field via the\n  guarded route in `WorldSafe`, and publish `source:\"backing-field\"`).\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 2 — COMMENT #5's \"WHAT IS MISSING\" #2 IS ALREADY SHIPPED\n=====================================================================\n\n\"**AutoRimmer cannot toggle auto-arm at all.** The checkbox lives in the Assign\ntab, which is exactly where 3.4's `assign` verb already works, so\n`assign {auto_arm:true}` is the natural home.\"\n\nThat landed in session 8, git-bug 1a072fa. `Source/AutoRimmer/PawnManageVerbs.cs`\ntakes `auto_arm` as an `assign` argument, cites both widget gates\n(`PawnColumnWorker_AutoArm.HasCheckbox` and the pawn gizmo's mech route) and\nechoes `auto_arm` in before/after on every `assign` call;\n`Source/AutoRimmer/FswaBridge.cs` is the bridge. The predicted home was exactly\nright — which is worth recording — but a 4.1 worker reading this comment today\nwould re-file work that is in the built DLL.\n\nThe same comment's alternative — \"AutoRimmer stops delegating and equips\nexplicitly with 3.4's `equip`\" — is also no longer future tense: `equip` is in the\nshipped verb list (`Source/AutoRimmer/`), alongside `wear`, `drop` and `carry`.\nSo BOTH branches of that decision are available today and the choice is a\nplaybook policy call, not a blocked dependency. That is a materially better\nposition than the comment describes.\n\nWhat in comment #5 remains TRUE and unbuilt: `WeaponPreference` really is\nmod-wide. `findsuitableweaponandammo/.../FSWAMod.cs` declares\n`public enum WeaponPreference { PreferRanged, RangedOnly, HighestDps }` — exactly\nthree values, no per-pawn variant — and `FSWA_MapComponent` reads\n`WeaponPreference preference = FSWAMod.Settings.weaponPreference;`. So the\nper-pawn melee/ranged decision still cannot be expressed to FSWA. CONFIRMED.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTION 3 — SMALL, ONE SEVERITY LABEL\n=====================================================================\n\nComment #1: \"`GameCondition_ToxicFallout.Init()` and `IncidentWorker_AggressiveAnimals`\nboth eagerly teach `ForbiddingDoors`+`AllowedAreas` at Critical\".\n\nHalf right. Verbatim:\n\n    GameCondition_ToxicFallout:      ForbiddingDoors Critical / AllowedAreas Critical\n    IncidentWorker_AggressiveAnimals: ForbiddingDoors Critical / AllowedAreas Important\n\nOnly toxic fallout treats allowed areas as Critical. The lesson (\"respond at\nincident start, not aftermath\") is unaffected — but if the plan is to mine the\nconcept defs as seed lessons at a known severity, as this issue proposes, then\nthe severity has to be read per call site, not per concept. A single concept can\nbe taught at two different tiers by two different incidents. Worth saying in the\nmining procedure.\n\n=====================================================================\nCONFIRMED, AND SEVERAL ARE STRONGER THAN THE ISSUE CLAIMS\n=====================================================================\n\n`RimWorld/Alert_NeedDefenses.GetReport` — CONFIRMED verbatim:\n`if (GenDate.DaysPassed \u003c 2 || GenDate.DaysPassed \u003e 5) { return false; }`.\nSilent from day 6, exactly as claimed. STRONGER THAN STATED: its structural test\nin `NeedDefenses` is satisfied by a SINGLE building —\n`building.def.building.IsTurret || isTrap || def == ThingDefOf.Sandbags ||\ndef == ThingDefOf.Barricade` — anywhere on any player-home map. So one sandbag\nsilences it permanently even inside the day-2-to-5 window. A structural\nreplacement should count, not test for presence.\n\n`RimWorld/Alert_NeedMealSource.NeedMealSource` — CONFIRMED: the only test is\n`if (allBuildingsColonist[i].def.building.isMealSource) { return false; }`.\nNOT IN THE ISSUE, AND IT IS THE SAME TRAP AS NEEDDEFENSES: `GetReport` also opens\nwith `if (GenDate.DaysPassed \u003c 2) { return false; }`. So this is a second case-2\nalert, time-scoped at the front. There may be more; the amendment names two and\nI found a third by reading, so the mining pass should grep every `Alert_*` for\n`GenDate.DaysPassed` rather than trusting the list.\n\n`RimWorld/Alert_FireInHomeArea.FireInHomeArea` — CONFIRMED home-scoped:\n`if (maps[i].areaManager.Home[thing.Position] \u0026\u0026 !thing.Position.Fogged(thing.Map))`.\nIt is also `ThingsOfDef(ThingDefOf.Fire)`, so it misses a modded fire def, which\nis why AutoRimmer's own `fires` scan uses `ThingRequestGroup.Fire`. Both reasons\nfor the checklist item, not one.\n\n`RimWorld/Alert_NeedWarmClothes` — CONFIRMED exactly:\n`private const int CheckNextTwelfthsCount = 3;`, `LowestTemperatureComing` walks\n`twelfth.NextTwelfth()` three times, and `GetTemperature` is\n`return GenTemperature.AverageTemperatureAtTileForTwelfth(map.Tile, twelfth);`.\nA GENUINE LEADING INDICATOR, as claimed. NEW TRAP, NOT IN THE ISSUE:\n`AnyColonistsNeedWarmClothes` counts only apparel for which\n`tmpApparelList[i].IsInAnyStorage()` holds. A parka lying on the ground — the\nnormal state of raider drops and of a drop-pod start — does NOT count as\navailable, so the alert can fire with warm clothes twenty cells away. That pairs\ndirectly with the \"forbidden sweep on day 0\" item and with comment #5's standing\norder about unforbidding drops; they are the same underlying fact seen twice.\nAlso: it is skipped for `map.Biome.inVacuum`, and suppressed unless\n`lowestTemperatureComing \u003c 5f`.\n\n`RimWorld/CompRottable`, the `SpoilageAndFreezers` teach — CONFIRMED and LATER\nthan claimed. It is not \"after food rotted\"; it is inside\n`if (Stage == RotStage.Rotting \u0026\u0026 PropsRot.rotDestroys)`, in the branch that\nsends `\"MessageRottedAwayInStorage\"` and then calls `parent.Destroy()`. The food\nis GONE at that point. And it is taught at `OpportunityType.GoodToKnow` — the\nlowest tier — which is itself the argument for the checklist originating its own\nsignal here.\n\n`RimWorld/OpportunityType` — CONFIRMED: `{ GoodToKnow, Important, Critical }`,\nthree values, a `byte` enum. Reusable as the confidence/severity vocabulary as\nthe amendment proposes.\n\n`RimWorld/IncidentWorker_Raid` — CONFIRMED:\n`LessonAutoActivator.TeachOpportunity(ConceptDefOf.EquippingWeapons, OpportunityType.Critical);`\nand, a few lines later,\n`LessonAutoActivator.TeachOpportunity(ConceptDefOf.ShieldBelts, OpportunityType.Critical);`.\nSo the raid path itself teaches the shield-belt concept at Critical, which\nconnects the \"armed colonists\" item and the \"shield belt + ranged weapon\" item —\nthey are the same vanilla moment.\n\nShield-belt alerts — CONFIRMED, there are exactly two:\n`RimWorld/Alert_ShieldUserHasRangedWeapon` and\n`RimWorld/Alert_HunterHasShieldAndRangedWeapon`.\n\n`Data/Core/Defs/Tutor/Instructions.xml` — CONFIRMED. The full defName sequence in\nfile order, from the Steam install, so the worker does not have to re-extract it:\n\n  CreateWorldParams, ChooseLandingSite, ReorderCharacters, RandomizeCharacter,\n  AcceptStartingPawns, MapCameraDolly, MapCameraZoom, Pause, TimeControls,\n  MakeStockpile, EndStockpileDesignating, UnforbidStartingResources,\n  BuildRoomWalls, BuildRoomDoor, BuildBeds, BuildTorchLamp,\n  BuildAnimalSleepingSpot, BuildHorseshoesPin, MakeGrowingZone, SetToGrowRice,\n  EquipWeapons, BuildFueledStove, AddBillSimpleMeal, MineSteel, ChopTrees,\n  OpenMainTabWork, ChangeWorkSettings, BuildSimpleResearchBench,\n  OpenMainTabResearch, SelectResearchProject, ExpandAreaHome,\n  ExpandAreaBuildRoof, BuildSandbags, FinishSandbags, DownRaider, UndraftAll,\n  ChooseStoryteller\n\n  The amendment's paraphrase is accurate. Two notes: `Pause`/`TimeControls`\n  precede the stockpile and are not camera or world-gen steps, so the trim\n  dropped two real steps; and the amendment's \"on raid: pause, read, draft, move\n  onto cover\" is one def (`DownRaider`), not four. `SetToGrowRice` really is a\n  separate step from `MakeGrowingZone` — which is the tutorial agreeing with\n  Correction 1 above, from the other direction.\n\n`findsuitableweaponandammo/.../FSWA_MapComponent.cs` — CONFIRMED, with the quote\nslightly abridged. The real sequence has a line between the two the issue quotes:\n\n    bool brawler = !pawn.RaceProps.IsMechanoid\n        \u0026\u0026 pawn.story?.traits?.HasTrait(TraitDefOf.Brawler) == true;\n    WeaponPreference preference = FSWAMod.Settings.weaponPreference;\n    bool allowRanged = !brawler;\n    bool allowMelee = brawler || preference != WeaponPreference.RangedOnly;\n\n  The omitted line is the one that PROVES the issue's own point 1, so it is worth\n  restoring in the playbook lesson. And the forbidden-weapon skip is not one call\n  site but THREE in that file: the candidate scan\n  (`thing.def.IsWeapon \u0026\u0026 !thing.IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)`), a per-weapon\n  check (`weapon.IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer) || weapon.IsBurning()`), and a\n  pinned-weapon check (`pinned.IsForbidden(Faction.OfPlayer)`). The standing order\n  is right and better supported than stated.\n\nComment #4's medical-bill lesson — CONFIRMED, AND IT NOW HAS SOURCE BACKING IT\nDID NOT HAVE. It was filed as a live observation. Both halves resolve to members:\n\n  * \"priority 0 means UNCHECKED, 3 means CHECKED\" — `RimWorld/WidgetsWork.DrawWorkBoxFor`,\n    the `!Find.PlaySettings.useWorkPriorities` branch, verbatim:\n        if (p.workSettings.GetPriority(wType) \u003e 0)\n        { p.workSettings.SetPriority(wType, 0); ... }\n        else { p.workSettings.SetPriority(wType, 3); ... }\n  * \"The patient does not count\" — `RimWorld/WorkGiver_DoBill.ShouldSkip`,\n    verbatim:\n        if (list[i] is IBillGiver billGiver \u0026\u0026 billGiver != pawn\n            \u0026\u0026 ThingIsUsableBillGiver(list[i]) \u0026\u0026 billGiver.BillStack.AnyShouldDoNow)\n        { return false; }\n        ...\n        return true;\n    `billGiver != pawn`. If the ONLY bill giver with a pending bill is the pawn\n    itself, `ShouldSkip` returns TRUE, the scanner never runs, and no job is ever\n    produced — which is precisely \"nothing looks broken, it just silently never\n    starts\", now explained rather than merely observed. Promote this lesson from\n    observation to source-cited; it is the strongest item on the issue.\n\n    (Incidental, and it belongs on 4.4's budget conversation rather than here:\n    that same line reaches `BillStack.AnyShouldDoNow` -\u003e `Bill_Production.ShouldDoNow`,\n    which the DESIGN log already records as writing the scribed `paused` flag.)\n\n=====================================================================\nTWO DEPENDENCIES IN THE BODY ARE STALE — THE READS ALREADY EXIST\n=====================================================================\n\n- \"Bill coverage ... Check bill count \u003e 0, not suspended, sane ingredient radius.\n  **Needs 3.6 (48f666c) first.**\" The READ ships today: `bills` is a shipped verb\n  (`Source/AutoRimmer/ColonyVerbs.Bills`), and per the DESIGN decisions log it\n  already publishes `next_ingredient_search_tick` precisely so \"active but worked\n  by nobody\" is observable. 3.6 is needed to CREATE and EDIT bills, not to check\n  coverage. The checklist item can be authored now.\n- \"Armed colonists ... **Needs 2.2's equipment field.**\" Also shipped:\n  `pawn {sections:[\"equipment\"]}` is served by `PawnSerializer.Equipment`, with a\n  `primary` flag per item. Authorable now.\n\nBoth matter because 4.1 is a wave-4 spec whose value is partly that the checklist\ncan be written before the verbs it will eventually use. Two of its items are\nmis-marked as blocked.\n\n=====================================================================\nINFERENCE, UNMARKED — WHERE THE BODY MIXES KINDS\n=====================================================================\n\nThe issue is unusually good about this; comment #5 explicitly says \"THE\nCALIBRATION STILL NEEDS EVAN\" and refuses to invent a formula, which is the right\nhandling. Two places where the marking is missing:\n\n- Comment #2's two seed lessons (\"colonists don't put down trees themselves\";\n  \"make sure a workbench is indoors\") are stated as flat game facts. They are\n  Evan's game knowledge, which is the correct SOURCE, but the body presents them\n  in the same register as the source-cited audit findings a comment away. Only the\n  second one carries \"the MECHANISMS exist ... but the RULE is playbook material\",\n  which is the distinction the whole issue is about. Label the first the same way.\n  I did not attempt to verify either from source and both are, I think,\n  unverifiable from source by construction — they are policy, not mechanism.\n- Comment #1's framing sentence, \"the game ships its own curriculum ... which is\n  the developers' explicit statement of what a player must anticipate\", is an\n  interpretation presented as a premise. It is a good interpretation and I would\n  keep it; it should read as one.\n\n=====================================================================\nACCEPTANCE — TESTABLE, WITH ONE UNCHECKABLE CLAUSE\n=====================================================================\n\n\"Dry-run: given a scripted journal from a lost colony ... following postmortem.md\nyields a correct lesson file AND a checklist item AND a template patch\" is\ntestable and, notably, needs NO bench: a synthetic journal is explicitly allowed,\nso this is a desk exercise. Good.\n\n\"**a fresh session's documented load order would surface all three**\" is not\ncheckable as written — \"would surface\" has no procedure and no observer. Make it\nmechanical: the session-start contract names an ordered file list; assert that\neach of the three artifacts appears in that list, at a named position, and that\n`playbook/INDEX.md` references the new lesson. That turns it into something a\nreviewer can fail.\n\nOne more, and it is the 3.4-fixture-table failure mode in advance: \"fire spread\nfrom unpoppered generator — construct via dev verbs or synthetically\" leaves the\nchoice open, and the two routes have different costs. The dev-verb route needs\n`dev:spawn-thing` for a generator, a fuel/short-circuit path, and an advance long\nenough for spread — none of which is a one-liner. Recommend pinning it to\nSYNTHETIC for this acceptance and noting that the dev-verb version is 4.3's job,\nwhere a real fire is wanted anyway.\n\n=====================================================================\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n=====================================================================\n\n- That the potato default is what the bench would actually show. I read the\n  getter; I did not create a zone and read it back. The counterfactual is one\n  bench call if you want it: make a growing zone with no plant, then `zones`.\n- That there are no further time-scoped alerts beyond the three now known\n  (`NeedDefenses`, `NeedMealSource`, and whatever else). I found the third by\n  reading two files, not by sweeping all of `Alert_*`. The sweep is worth doing\n  and I did not do it.\n- Everything about how the lessons should be WORDED. Those are recommendations.","files":null}]}